(2440241, 332216, 53572, '<td valign="top">this is a stunning image.  just lovely.  tones, focus, exposure, composition - all spot on.  if you ever have this as a print, i''m there, man!</td>', 'this is a stunning image.  just lovely.  tones, focus, exposure, composition - all spot on.  if you ever have this as a print, i''m there, man!', '2006-05-20 13:10:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(2440407, 329958, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)</b> <br/> <br/>For a first shot this is an excellent attempt at capturing a very difficult image. The problem with the moon is the fact that it remains quite bright and digital camera can barely cope with dark surroundings and a sudden light source (I am aware that the moon reflects sunlight and does not emit it, but for arguments sake, I''ll call it a light source). So all in all, this shot works very effectively. Your 1/250 shutter speed was definitely a good call. <br/> <br/>There isn''t a lot that could be improved in this image. I think that, as someone else noted earlier, possibly converting to black and white would have been good as it would have taken away some of that unavoidable colour noise around the edges. In terms of focus there isn''t much that one can do without a huge telephoto lens. You''ve actually managed to capture this one surprisingly well; I''m guessing you used the manual focus on your camera otherwise this is rather impressive for a cannon.<br/> <br/>I think the reason this shot didn''t go further was because of its relevance to the competition. I mean, I can''t think of one more adequate but people tended to go for the landscapes rather then the single shots. My point is that while this is technically a very fine photo, it seems to be lacking content as it only shows one subject that we all know very well.<br/> <br/>Overall, the photo is great and you should be proud of it. It''s one of the best moon shots I''ve seen with a digital camera.<br/> <br/>Good Luck with future competitions!<br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·.</b></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)  For a first shot this is an excellent attempt at capturing a very difficult image. The problem with the moon is the fact that it remains quite bright and digital camera can barely cope with dark surroundings and a sudden light source (I am aware that the moon reflects sunlight and does not emit it, but for arguments sake, I''ll call it a light source). So all in all, this shot works very effectively. Your 1/250 shutter speed was definitely a good call.  There isn''t a lot that could be improved in this image. I think that, as someone else noted earlier, possibly converting to black and white would have been good as it would have taken away some of that unavoidable colour noise around the edges. In terms of focus there isn''t much that one can do without a huge telephoto lens. You''ve actually managed to capture this one surprisingly well; I''m guessing you used the manual focus on your camera otherwise this is rather impressive for a cannon. I think the reason this shot didn''t go further was because of its relevance to the competition. I mean, I can''t think of one more adequate but people tended to go for the landscapes rather then the single shots. My point is that while this is technically a very fine photo, it seems to be lacking content as it only shows one subject that we all know very well. Overall, the photo is great and you should be proud of it. It''s one of the best moon shots I''ve seen with a digital camera. Good Luck with future competitions! .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·.', '2006-05-20 14:20:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(2440559, 331858, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE <br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>My first impression is "Cool."  I really like the contrast of the orange supports and the blue sky.  This is begging to be photographed again. . .<br/> <br/>Compositionally, it seems a bit random to me, without a definite area for the eyes to "rest" on.  The eyes are torn from looking to the "end of the tunnel" and the awesome randomness at top.  That can be good in that it really sets a "mood" for the picture, and the puzzlement at teh top adds to the confusion the composition sets up.<br/> <br/>Technically, the blown out spot on the left really spoils an otherwise great effect.  Couldn''t you ahve shot the light out with a bb gun, or sling shot or soemthing.  (I AM JUST KIDDING).  that being said, while the handrails make for some interesting leading lines, cropping the bottom half out sets up for a neat abstract.<br/> <br/>Good work!<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE by karmat My first impression is "Cool."  I really like the contrast of the orange supports and the blue sky.  This is begging to be photographed again. . . Compositionally, it seems a bit random to me, without a definite area for the eyes to "rest" on.  The eyes are torn from looking to the "end of the tunnel" and the awesome randomness at top.  That can be good in that it really sets a "mood" for the picture, and the puzzlement at teh top adds to the confusion the composition sets up. Technically, the blown out spot on the left really spoils an otherwise great effect.  Couldn''t you ahve shot the light out with a bb gun, or sling shot or soemthing.  (I AM JUST KIDDING).  that being said, while the handrails make for some interesting leading lines, cropping the bottom half out sets up for a neat abstract. Good work! karmat', '2006-05-20 14:53:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(2440595, 332007, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)</b> <br/> <br/>Wow, this is a truly incredible shot. There really isn''t a lot that I can say in terms of improving the photograph itself. The colours are fantastic and the setting is ideal to show them off, with a clear subject and great DOF. Going through this competition I''ve seen a lot of grainy images when digital cameras overcompensate, but here it has all come out perfectly, proving your knowledge around a camera. The 2.5 shutter speed was certainly an excellent choice and seems to be the primary aspect that puts this image head and shoulders above the others in this competition.<br/> <br/>In terms of any negative points, and there really are very few, I think that the image might be a little too chaotic for some. The boats, string of lights, reflections, and surrounding buildings slightly crowd the lights coming from the central subject. Also, the colourful and vibrant blues and oranges make the boats a little yellowish, which I find slightly distracting. <br/> <br/>However, with that said, none of the composition is really avoidable and I''m very surprised that this photo didn''t actually go much further in the competition. <br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions!<br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·.</b> <br/></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)  Wow, this is a truly incredible shot. There really isn''t a lot that I can say in terms of improving the photograph itself. The colours are fantastic and the setting is ideal to show them off, with a clear subject and great DOF. Going through this competition I''ve seen a lot of grainy images when digital cameras overcompensate, but here it has all come out perfectly, proving your knowledge around a camera. The 2.5 shutter speed was certainly an excellent choice and seems to be the primary aspect that puts this image head and shoulders above the others in this competition. In terms of any negative points, and there really are very few, I think that the image might be a little too chaotic for some. The boats, string of lights, reflections, and surrounding buildings slightly crowd the lights coming from the central subject. Also, the colourful and vibrant blues and oranges make the boats a little yellowish, which I find slightly distracting.  However, with that said, none of the composition is really avoidable and I''m very surprised that this photo didn''t actually go much further in the competition.  Good Luck for all Future Competitions! .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·. ', '2006-05-20 15:03:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2440697, 330840, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi pieterw2008 :)<br/> <br/>The composition of this photo is excellent, and the exposure also creates a nice sense of sound and motion for me.  I think you did an excellent job with this image, and it appears that the voters feel the same way :)<br/> <br/>As I was reading your information about post processing, I may be able to offer you some assistance in ''perfecting'' this photograph.  It seems that the orange glow of the light wasn''t your preference, so learning to shift the white balance may be useful to you.  If you are shooting in raw mode, you can shift the camera white balance after the shot during the raw conversion process.  If not, you can use the levels dialog box in photoshop to select a poing within the image that is supposed to be ''white'' and it will do some color correction for you.  This option doesn''t always work out well, but it can in a lot of cases.  Your third option is to make it a black and white :)  My personal preference in photography is black and white.  If I create a photo where the color is not playing a key role, it ends up as a black and white anyway :)<br/> <br/>Excellent work...<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi pieterw2008 :) The composition of this photo is excellent, and the exposure also creates a nice sense of sound and motion for me.  I think you did an excellent job with this image, and it appears that the voters feel the same way :) As I was reading your information about post processing, I may be able to offer you some assistance in ''perfecting'' this photograph.  It seems that the orange glow of the light wasn''t your preference, so learning to shift the white balance may be useful to you.  If you are shooting in raw mode, you can shift the camera white balance after the shot during the raw conversion process.  If not, you can use the levels dialog box in photoshop to select a poing within the image that is supposed to be ''white'' and it will do some color correction for you.  This option doesn''t always work out well, but it can in a lot of cases.  Your third option is to make it a black and white :)  My personal preference in photography is black and white.  If I create a photo where the color is not playing a key role, it ends up as a black and white anyway :) Excellent work... John Setzler', '2006-05-20 15:53:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2440901, 331990, 33515, '<td valign="top">Very nice finish Becky, congrats on the top 10</td>', 'Very nice finish Becky, congrats on the top 10', '2006-05-20 17:49:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(2441288, 330827, 53147, '<td valign="top">Trading Post - <br/>Congrats on you rnew high score - that always feels great. It looks like for some reason I did not vote on this. I think I would have given it a 6. I have no experience with long shutter speeds and would not have guessed this to be taken too late into the night. The lighting works very well and I like the variety of textures that you have within the frame. I am not so sure about the big bar down on the bottom. Otherwise a really good shot - well done.</td>', 'Trading Post - Congrats on you rnew high score - that always feels great. It looks like for some reason I did not vote on this. I think I would have given it a 6. I have no experience with long shutter speeds and would not have guessed this to be taken too late into the night. The lighting works very well and I like the variety of textures that you have within the frame. I am not so sure about the big bar down on the bottom. Otherwise a really good shot - well done.', '2006-05-20 19:57:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2441307, 332081, 53147, '<td valign="top">Trading POst - <br/>Doesnt look like anyone took heed to your request in the thread - so I will join the pack. I may have cropped out the piece of light that shows up on the left - keeping that side as complete shadow. With that mayeb rotating the shot so that the left line of the building is parallel to the frame. Sharpness and detail i nthe building is good. Overall not a really bad shot - just not very interesting in general.</td>', 'Trading POst - Doesnt look like anyone took heed to your request in the thread - so I will join the pack. I may have cropped out the piece of light that shows up on the left - keeping that side as complete shadow. With that mayeb rotating the shot so that the left line of the building is parallel to the frame. Sharpness and detail i nthe building is good. Overall not a really bad shot - just not very interesting in general.', '2006-05-20 20:05:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2441315, 332313, 53147, '<td valign="top">Tradingh post - <br/>Look slike if I hadnt given you that 7 I would have beaten you by a bit. We have a nice little thing going seeing each other on the same page. Very nice shot. Dont really know what I would do to this to make it better. Maybe just a tad higher saturation to make the colors a bit more vibrant - but thats a big maybe. You have a great shot here and it makes me want to go out and see if I could capture something like this. Well done.</td>', 'Tradingh post - Look slike if I hadnt given you that 7 I would have beaten you by a bit. We have a nice little thing going seeing each other on the same page. Very nice shot. Dont really know what I would do to this to make it better. Maybe just a tad higher saturation to make the colors a bit more vibrant - but thats a big maybe. You have a great shot here and it makes me want to go out and see if I could capture something like this. Well done.', '2006-05-20 20:12:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(2441407, 332243, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First off, congrats on your growing scores.  Your last two are on an upward trend.  Second, welcome to DPC.  I hope to see those scores keep on growing.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> Nice choice of settings to give this shot the slight underexposure that it needed.  The tree line, as well as the power lines are nicely focused.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> Really cool lighting.  This gives are great moodiness to the capture, and gives the viewer a sense of anticipation that all daylight is about to fade away.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> Beautiful sunset, and I like the composition.  You are correct that the title threw the voters off.  I imagine that it did cost you more than you think.  Lots of voters are tough on titles, a lesson I learned the hard way.  While I usually ignore the title all together, most around here do not.  I do not think that there is too much sky included.  I think that it serves well in providing a greater tonal range.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> I think that you would have approached a score or 5.8-6.0 with a more clever title.  Again, voters will tear you apart for a title they think is off or wrong.  Nice capture that fits the challenge well.  Congrats on your best score to date, and we look forward to more of the upward trend.</td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  First off, congrats on your growing scores.  Your last two are on an upward trend.  Second, welcome to DPC.  I hope to see those scores keep on growing. Camera Work/Technical: Nice choice of settings to give this shot the slight underexposure that it needed.  The tree line, as well as the power lines are nicely focused. Lighting: Really cool lighting.  This gives are great moodiness to the capture, and gives the viewer a sense of anticipation that all daylight is about to fade away. Composition/Content: Beautiful sunset, and I like the composition.  You are correct that the title threw the voters off.  I imagine that it did cost you more than you think.  Lots of voters are tough on titles, a lesson I learned the hard way.  While I usually ignore the title all together, most around here do not.  I do not think that there is too much sky included.  I think that it serves well in providing a greater tonal range. My Opinion: I think that you would have approached a score or 5.8-6.0 with a more clever title.  Again, voters will tear you apart for a title they think is off or wrong.  Nice capture that fits the challenge well.  Congrats on your best score to date, and we look forward to more of the upward trend.', '2006-05-20 21:03:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2441486, 332477, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> This one is very hard to critique in the normal sense of a photograph critique.  Nothing is very focused outside the glass, but I am sure that it was the effect that you intended when you captured the image.  I cannot tell you that I think it is wrong, it''s jut very difficult to offer any suggestions.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> I like the lighting.  It adds to the overall feel of the image, and works very nicely with the scene you captured.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> You created a very interesting feel in this capture, but not one that many voters identified with.  <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> I like it in one sense, but I don''t think that I would have scored it very highly.  It is often difficult to merge the photographs that we like to take with the photographs that we enter into challenges here.  I am assuming that by entering it, you wanted to score highly with it.  That is where the balance and compromise comes into play.  Voters here like vivid colors, nice composition, and super-crisp images.  For a challenge entry, this one really had none of those elements.  For a collection, this one would work nicely.<br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  Camera Work/Technical: This one is very hard to critique in the normal sense of a photograph critique.  Nothing is very focused outside the glass, but I am sure that it was the effect that you intended when you captured the image.  I cannot tell you that I think it is wrong, it''s jut very difficult to offer any suggestions. Lighting: I like the lighting.  It adds to the overall feel of the image, and works very nicely with the scene you captured. Composition/Content: You created a very interesting feel in this capture, but not one that many voters identified with.   My Opinion: I like it in one sense, but I don''t think that I would have scored it very highly.  It is often difficult to merge the photographs that we like to take with the photographs that we enter into challenges here.  I am assuming that by entering it, you wanted to score highly with it.  That is where the balance and compromise comes into play.  Voters here like vivid colors, nice composition, and super-crisp images.  For a challenge entry, this one really had none of those elements.  For a collection, this one would work nicely.', '2006-05-20 21:35:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(2441644, 330712, 41337, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club... </b> <br/> <br/>I like it. This image simply says "night" to me so good job capturing that. The colors have that night feeling but it''s carried off in a rather unique way I must say. All in all everything about this works. <br/> <br/>One thing I think could have improved this was to have a better scene in the mirror. If those people were with you having one of them looking into that car would have made the scene in the mirror easier to identify or imagine. <br/> <br/>That''s pretty much it. Technically, I don''t have any real problems with this. Maybe have the pole and sign a bit more sharp but other than that I wouldn''t change anything. I liked the idea behind this and you hit the challenge theme perfectly so congrats on a fine image.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-20 22:02:27.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club...   I like it. This image simply says "night" to me so good job capturing that. The colors have that night feeling but it''s carried off in a rather unique way I must say. All in all everything about this works.  One thing I think could have improved this was to have a better scene in the mirror. If those people were with you having one of them looking into that car would have made the scene in the mirror easier to identify or imagine.  That''s pretty much it. Technically, I don''t have any real problems with this. Maybe have the pole and sign a bit more sharp but other than that I wouldn''t change anything. I liked the idea behind this and you hit the challenge theme perfectly so congrats on a fine image.', '2006-05-20 23:00:36.000000', '2006-05-20 23:02:27.000000', 0),
(2441710, 332214, 41337, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/>First off, let me say welcome to DPC! I see this was your first challenge you entered. I hope there will be many more that follow. Below is my critique of your photo. Please take these as suggestions/things to consider. <br/> <br/><b>Technicals:</b> <br/>I''m not sure exactly what your intentions were but I think you wanted to capture the near full moon. If so your exposure time was way too much as everything in the sky got blown out. Try a much faster shutter speed (say 3 seconds to start out with) and an aperture setting around 4 or 5.6 to give you a bit more DOF. I''d rather have a image on the dark side than on the bright side since darker images are easier to fix in post processing so keep that in mind when experiementing with your settings.<br/> <br/><b>Subject/composition:</b> <br/>The trees are a real distraction. Getting a good exposure of the night sky with the trees that close may be next to impossible so I''d suggest composing this shot different so any trees in the shot would be at a greater distance. That way any wind effects would be far less noticable. Also, I''d suggest framing the subject off-center. I assume the moon is in that bright spot so when you frame this have it more to the left preferrably.<br/> <br/><b>Verdict/Summary:</b> <br/>The image suffers greatly due to the technicals. With time I think you can get a sharper cleaner image. Even if you wanted that bright glow in the sky the trees that close will continue to give you problems so have those at a greater distance and your image will come across better.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   First off, let me say welcome to DPC! I see this was your first challenge you entered. I hope there will be many more that follow. Below is my critique of your photo. Please take these as suggestions/things to consider.  Technicals: I''m not sure exactly what your intentions were but I think you wanted to capture the near full moon. If so your exposure time was way too much as everything in the sky got blown out. Try a much faster shutter speed (say 3 seconds to start out with) and an aperture setting around 4 or 5.6 to give you a bit more DOF. I''d rather have a image on the dark side than on the bright side since darker images are easier to fix in post processing so keep that in mind when experiementing with your settings. Subject/composition: The trees are a real distraction. Getting a good exposure of the night sky with the trees that close may be next to impossible so I''d suggest composing this shot different so any trees in the shot would be at a greater distance. That way any wind effects would be far less noticable. Also, I''d suggest framing the subject off-center. I assume the moon is in that bright spot so when you frame this have it more to the left preferrably. Verdict/Summary: The image suffers greatly due to the technicals. With time I think you can get a sharper cleaner image. Even if you wanted that bright glow in the sky the trees that close will continue to give you problems so have those at a greater distance and your image will come across better.', '2006-05-20 23:28:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(2442179, 330844, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.  Thank you for the opportunity to criqitue your your first entry.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Interesting shot .. a little much to focus on ...<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>As mentioned there is a little much to focus on .. if you could have focused on a smaller area or on one or two of the tulips this would have created a more interesting and speicific image, but also created the focus point.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Meets the challenge .. and does so very well ...<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I think that the comments you have received is good .. and me giving you more will confuse the issue ... take note that the comments are really good on this.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Take a look at the other shots in the challenge .. and learn from them ... but also have a look aroudn at a few of the portfolios ... and try to learn from what they are doing.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>What a great first shot .. I remember mine ... not that long along ... a branch ... we all learn and this is a great place to do it.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.  Thank you for the opportunity to criqitue your your first entry. First Impression - the most important one: Interesting shot .. a little much to focus on ... Composition: As mentioned there is a little much to focus on .. if you could have focused on a smaller area or on one or two of the tulips this would have created a more interesting and speicific image, but also created the focus point. Subject: Meets the challenge .. and does so very well ... Technical (Colour and light): I think that the comments you have received is good .. and me giving you more will confuse the issue ... take note that the comments are really good on this. To grow its vote?: Take a look at the other shots in the challenge .. and learn from them ... but also have a look aroudn at a few of the portfolios ... and try to learn from what they are doing. Summary: What a great first shot .. I remember mine ... not that long along ... a branch ... we all learn and this is a great place to do it. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-05-21 03:33:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(2442188, 331679, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Interesting shot .. .not really what people would be looking for as a night shot ... I think that may be the issue with the scoring you reiceved.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>This is interesting to some and not others ... that could be the issue ..I think that it is a little small ... try to maximise your images to the maximum of 640 longest side .. to create a bigger impact.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Just because it is take at night .. doesn''t make a night shot .... yes it was taken at night .. and yes it met the challenge .. but the image doesn''t tell me that ...<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Interesting shot ... colour is good .. appears to be a little oversharpened .. I can make out the face .. and its cropping appears to meet the rule of thirds.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>I don''t know that this shots vote could be improved ... but I think that by taking a look at the other shots that have been taken and see what they did you will find other ways of interesting shots.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Well done, interesting shot ... I like what you have been doing ... keep it up .. <br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: Interesting shot .. .not really what people would be looking for as a night shot ... I think that may be the issue with the scoring you reiceved. Composition: This is interesting to some and not others ... that could be the issue ..I think that it is a little small ... try to maximise your images to the maximum of 640 longest side .. to create a bigger impact. Subject: Just because it is take at night .. doesn''t make a night shot .... yes it was taken at night .. and yes it met the challenge .. but the image doesn''t tell me that ... Technical (Colour and light): Interesting shot ... colour is good .. appears to be a little oversharpened .. I can make out the face .. and its cropping appears to meet the rule of thirds. To grow its vote?: I don''t know that this shots vote could be improved ... but I think that by taking a look at the other shots that have been taken and see what they did you will find other ways of interesting shots. Summary: Well done, interesting shot ... I like what you have been doing ... keep it up ..  If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-05-21 03:43:38.000000', NULL, 0),
