(2442435, 330840, 57260, '<td valign="top">Thank you for the feedback. I really appreciate it, seeing that I''m new at this.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-21 08:42:21.</i></td>', 'Thank you for the feedback. I really appreciate it, seeing that I''m new at this.', '2006-05-21 09:41:31.000000', '2006-05-21 09:42:21.000000', 0),
(2442792, 329909, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>This is a pretty straight forward shot to critique.  What you have here is a very nice capture of a lightning strike!  (I can hear ya sayin'', "Duh", but that''s OK!)  But that''s all it is.  A lightning strike.  This type of shot is like anyother type of shot.  It needs to follow the same types of ''rules of photography'' as any other type of shot.  For example:  A landscape shot needs a subject.  A leading line shot needs a subject.  A sunset shot needs a subject.  In general a landscape, leading line or sunset are not typically strong enough on their own to actually be the subject of the shot.  There are exceptions!  Ask BearMusic about landscapes without subjects and he can show you many examples that work, but they are exceptions, not the rule.<br/> <br/>This can be directly translated to this shot.  There is nothing in the shot to truly be a subject.  The lightning is nice, but not spectacular.  Add a silhouette or a city scape with the lightning behind it and you have something special.  Add a tree or house lit up by the lightning and you have something special.  What you have here is almost the same as a snapshot.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps you see why your shot topped out at 5.49...<br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: This is a pretty straight forward shot to critique.  What you have here is a very nice capture of a lightning strike!  (I can hear ya sayin'', "Duh", but that''s OK!)  But that''s all it is.  A lightning strike.  This type of shot is like anyother type of shot.  It needs to follow the same types of ''rules of photography'' as any other type of shot.  For example:  A landscape shot needs a subject.  A leading line shot needs a subject.  A sunset shot needs a subject.  In general a landscape, leading line or sunset are not typically strong enough on their own to actually be the subject of the shot.  There are exceptions!  Ask BearMusic about landscapes without subjects and he can show you many examples that work, but they are exceptions, not the rule. This can be directly translated to this shot.  There is nothing in the shot to truly be a subject.  The lightning is nice, but not spectacular.  Add a silhouette or a city scape with the lightning behind it and you have something special.  Add a tree or house lit up by the lightning and you have something special.  What you have here is almost the same as a snapshot. Hope this helps you see why your shot topped out at 5.49...YoursTC', '2006-05-21 13:11:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443056, 331869, 14414, '<td valign="top">I like this. Its busy, but not too crowded a composition. The viewpoint works well, even if it was the only angle you could get. I have to admit I don''t personally like the border - maybe I''ve been around dpc too long, ''cause I used to like that style of border. <br/>I can''t quite put my finger on it, but there''s something about the top railings that isnt quite right - possibly a little oversharpened?<br/> <br/>Its a good clean, dynamic image, deserving of its score/placing. I know you were stuck for choice with the angle, but I''d like it if there was nothing distracting behind in the b/g.</td>', 'I like this. Its busy, but not too crowded a composition. The viewpoint works well, even if it was the only angle you could get. I have to admit I don''t personally like the border - maybe I''ve been around dpc too long, ''cause I used to like that style of border. I can''t quite put my finger on it, but there''s something about the top railings that isnt quite right - possibly a little oversharpened? Its a good clean, dynamic image, deserving of its score/placing. I know you were stuck for choice with the angle, but I''d like it if there was nothing distracting behind in the b/g.', '2006-05-21 14:38:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443086, 330032, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)</b> <br/> <br/>Well, where to begin... The idea for this photo was a good one and I think that with a bit more careful technical expertise, it could have been a winning shot.<br/> <br/>The first problem is the grain in the image. This is commonly found when a digital camera takes a night shot. They tend to over-compensate and the result is a lot of ''noise'' in the image. To avoid this you can either just shoot in lighter conditions, or you can use a much longer shutter speed.<br/> <br/>The next thing is the camera shake. Always brace the camera when taking the picture, even if it''s just on a pile of books or someone''s shoulder; one has to do it to obtain a nice clear image.<br/> <br/>I think that once those two pointers were undertaken, you''d end up with a lovely crisp, clear shot that would have at least doubled the place you received. <br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions!<br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·.</b> <br/> <br/></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)  Well, where to begin... The idea for this photo was a good one and I think that with a bit more careful technical expertise, it could have been a winning shot. The first problem is the grain in the image. This is commonly found when a digital camera takes a night shot. They tend to over-compensate and the result is a lot of ''noise'' in the image. To avoid this you can either just shoot in lighter conditions, or you can use a much longer shutter speed. The next thing is the camera shake. Always brace the camera when taking the picture, even if it''s just on a pile of books or someone''s shoulder; one has to do it to obtain a nice clear image. I think that once those two pointers were undertaken, you''d end up with a lovely crisp, clear shot that would have at least doubled the place you received.  Good Luck for all Future Competitions! .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·.  ', '2006-05-21 14:48:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443148, 330138, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)</b> <br/> <br/>Ok, this is a nice place and has the opportunity for a great photo although there are a few pointers that could send you in the right direction.<br/> <br/>First of all, the camera you''re using is perfectly capable of making this a winning shot with a few small settings changed. A longer shutter speed would remove that grainy noise we can see in the sky and would help to bring out some more of the details found in this image. Be aware that digital cameras tend to falter in dark conditions when pointed directly at a light source. It completely confuses their internal processors. <br/> <br/>My next point would be the angle at which this was taken. At DPC, the voters ALWAYS pick up on crooked backgrounds and horizons. So either use a well-balanced tripod with the grid function activated on your camera, or rotate it afterwards in some photo editing software. I read on your money shot, that you do not use such programs, I don''t know if this is still true, but I would strongly suggest small touch ups to your entries if you''d like them to go further.<br/> <br/>I think that will all that said, and some minor improvements made, this photo could certainly get into the top quarter of the final results. <br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions!<br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·.</b></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)  Ok, this is a nice place and has the opportunity for a great photo although there are a few pointers that could send you in the right direction. First of all, the camera you''re using is perfectly capable of making this a winning shot with a few small settings changed. A longer shutter speed would remove that grainy noise we can see in the sky and would help to bring out some more of the details found in this image. Be aware that digital cameras tend to falter in dark conditions when pointed directly at a light source. It completely confuses their internal processors.  My next point would be the angle at which this was taken. At DPC, the voters ALWAYS pick up on crooked backgrounds and horizons. So either use a well-balanced tripod with the grid function activated on your camera, or rotate it afterwards in some photo editing software. I read on your money shot, that you do not use such programs, I don''t know if this is still true, but I would strongly suggest small touch ups to your entries if you''d like them to go further. I think that will all that said, and some minor improvements made, this photo could certainly get into the top quarter of the final results.  Good Luck for all Future Competitions! .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·.', '2006-05-21 15:14:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443167, 331625, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)</b> <br/> <br/>Isn''t it annoying when a photograph doesn''t do as well as you''d hoped and yet there are no negative comments? I find it drives me mad, so I''ll try and work out why this didn''t do as well as it should''ve.<br/> <br/>Your initial image is a fairly boring one with just a person walking down a pavement at dusk, but you''ve certainly added an interest twist to this one. Your slow shutter speed as unearthed new aspects that would have remained invisible before. I certainly like your depth of field in this image to keep the subject of the photograph in focus while giving the uneventful background some life. <br/> <br/>I think the only problem here is that voters tend to whiz through images in rapid succession and at a quick glance, this photo looks as though you don''t know how to use your Casio. Also, there seems to be a little too much going on in the frame for some people. The chaos factor implied in your picture tends to be a love/hate with many voters and so you might have been unfortunate enough to get the wrong mix of opinions.<br/> <br/>The incredible blueness of the sky here really helps energise the image, although I dislike the top right corner where there seems to have been a source of light when you took the photo. <br/> <br/>Overall, this is a perfectly fine photograph and is relevant to this competition. However, I think the reason it didn''t score higher lies in the region of people preferring much steadier and clearer landscapes as opposed to small subjects, and chaotic colours. <br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions!<br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·.</b> <br/></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)  Isn''t it annoying when a photograph doesn''t do as well as you''d hoped and yet there are no negative comments? I find it drives me mad, so I''ll try and work out why this didn''t do as well as it should''ve. Your initial image is a fairly boring one with just a person walking down a pavement at dusk, but you''ve certainly added an interest twist to this one. Your slow shutter speed as unearthed new aspects that would have remained invisible before. I certainly like your depth of field in this image to keep the subject of the photograph in focus while giving the uneventful background some life.  I think the only problem here is that voters tend to whiz through images in rapid succession and at a quick glance, this photo looks as though you don''t know how to use your Casio. Also, there seems to be a little too much going on in the frame for some people. The chaos factor implied in your picture tends to be a love/hate with many voters and so you might have been unfortunate enough to get the wrong mix of opinions. The incredible blueness of the sky here really helps energise the image, although I dislike the top right corner where there seems to have been a source of light when you took the photo.  Overall, this is a perfectly fine photograph and is relevant to this competition. However, I think the reason it didn''t score higher lies in the region of people preferring much steadier and clearer landscapes as opposed to small subjects, and chaotic colours.  Good Luck for all Future Competitions! .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·. ', '2006-05-21 15:26:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443169, 330827, 14414, '<td valign="top">This is one of the nightshot''s that stood out to me in the challenge - I like the subtle lighting and the composition. It doesnt give a big impression to me of nighttime, would be more suited to a candid challenge imho. <br/>The lighting is perfect, keeping nice soft tones and detail. The framing of the trees and the horizontal wall (?) along the bottom works well to contain the subject, but I would crop of a little of the bottom - maintaining the stripe, but just not so much ''wasted'' space. <br/> <br/>I have to admit the shadow on the wall is rather distracting - makes it look to me as thought you have shot through railings or something. But its a great shot nonetheless, congrats on the personal best :)</td>', 'This is one of the nightshot''s that stood out to me in the challenge - I like the subtle lighting and the composition. It doesnt give a big impression to me of nighttime, would be more suited to a candid challenge imho. The lighting is perfect, keeping nice soft tones and detail. The framing of the trees and the horizontal wall (?) along the bottom works well to contain the subject, but I would crop of a little of the bottom - maintaining the stripe, but just not so much ''wasted'' space.  I have to admit the shadow on the wall is rather distracting - makes it look to me as thought you have shot through railings or something. But its a great shot nonetheless, congrats on the personal best :)', '2006-05-21 15:27:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443355, 332313, 14414, '<td valign="top">Sorry to get to it so late - I started a comment a few days back but never finished it. <br/>This is a very crisp, strong cityscape. The car trails work well as a leading line, and the small aperture has worked wonders with the lights!<br/>The area of darkness in the bottom left makes the composition a little unbalanced (but I didn''t really notice it til the second time I looked at it). Conversely, the brighter area of streetlights on the right hand side works well as a compositional element right on the third.</td>', 'Sorry to get to it so late - I started a comment a few days back but never finished it. This is a very crisp, strong cityscape. The car trails work well as a leading line, and the small aperture has worked wonders with the lights!The area of darkness in the bottom left makes the composition a little unbalanced (but I didn''t really notice it til the second time I looked at it). Conversely, the brighter area of streetlights on the right hand side works well as a compositional element right on the third.', '2006-05-21 16:39:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443809, 331990, 40370, '<td valign="top">7 Seven Sieben Siete Sette ... Did I mention you have a 7 ... woohoo!!<br/> <br/>I enjoyed your "long story" thread regarding the recovery of this picture. It''s kind of a miracle picture that has proved to be extraordinary. 6th Place is quite the accomplishment. Hope to see you soon.</td>', '7 Seven Sieben Siete Sette ... Did I mention you have a 7 ... woohoo!! I enjoyed your "long story" thread regarding the recovery of this picture. It''s kind of a miracle picture that has proved to be extraordinary. 6th Place is quite the accomplishment. Hope to see you soon.', '2006-05-21 19:37:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443926, 330271, 59278, '<td valign="top">I like it. But on the D50, because of the city lights I would of set the white balance to incandescent, that would of gave you the correct color.</td>', 'I like it. But on the D50, because of the city lights I would of set the white balance to incandescent, that would of gave you the correct color.', '2006-05-21 20:04:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(2443961, 332313, 51802, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>Well it looks like you have received a lot of good feedback already.  I agree with the comments regarding the lower left corner.  Maybe a slightly different camera position would have helped with that.  I very much like what appears to be turn signals in the middle of the oncoming traffic.  I haven''t seen that before in a shot like this.<br/> <br/>Well done.<br/>-Bill</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club!  Well it looks like you have received a lot of good feedback already.  I agree with the comments regarding the lower left corner.  Maybe a slightly different camera position would have helped with that.  I very much like what appears to be turn signals in the middle of the oncoming traffic.  I haven''t seen that before in a shot like this. Well done.-Bill', '2006-05-21 20:16:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(2444125, 331974, 47933, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club Alexej!<br/> <br/>First impression: Great shot. <br/> <br/>This is a difficult image for me to give a long critique for as there isn''t much I would''ve done differently. A tighter crop on the right would''ve gotten rid of the distracting neon sign. It really pulls your eye away from the subject. If you''d have cropped it tighter on the left you''d have taken the hotel sign out but the people as well. I think the shot needs the people so I think it''s good over there.<br/> <br/>Overall a very nice shot and probably would''ve scored better if there wasn''t a similar image entered in the same challenge.<br/> <br/>Thanks for the opportunity to critique this shot Alexej.<br/> <br/>Cheers - Phil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Alexej! First impression: Great shot.  This is a difficult image for me to give a long critique for as there isn''t much I would''ve done differently. A tighter crop on the right would''ve gotten rid of the distracting neon sign. It really pulls your eye away from the subject. If you''d have cropped it tighter on the left you''d have taken the hotel sign out but the people as well. I think the shot needs the people so I think it''s good over there. Overall a very nice shot and probably would''ve scored better if there wasn''t a similar image entered in the same challenge. Thanks for the opportunity to critique this shot Alexej. Cheers - Phil', '2006-05-21 21:15:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2444177, 331990, 47117, '<td valign="top">Wonderful image and perspective.  I nined it!</td>', 'Wonderful image and perspective.  I nined it!', '2006-05-21 21:30:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2444911, 331899, 30982, '<td valign="top">Per your comments, I actually prefer this version with the distortion over the one that''s straightened post processing.  The "distortion" kind of adds to the picture in my humble opinion.<br/> <br/>Awesome picture - I can easily picture this as a full-page color shot in a travel brochure!  Congrats on the ribbon, too!</td>', 'Per your comments, I actually prefer this version with the distortion over the one that''s straightened post processing.  The "distortion" kind of adds to the picture in my humble opinion. Awesome picture - I can easily picture this as a full-page color shot in a travel brochure!  Congrats on the ribbon, too!', '2006-05-22 00:39:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2444946, 332205, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique.  Secondly, congrats on your highest scoring challenge entry to date.  Looking at your profile, it seems that you are on a steady incline to the top.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> This is one of the better moon shots that I have seen in some time.  I like the choice of WB you used here, giving the photo a warmer feel than the normal white moon.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> Great work capturing only the brightness of the moon without overexposing it, and without exposing anything else in the sky.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> Wonderful placement to make for a very nice composition.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> While a very nice capture, moon photos usually don''t do very well in challenges here.  I think this one scored to its full potential on this site.  It is really hard to get a decent composition that affords a size large enough to include the right amount of detail.  Nice capture.<br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique.  Secondly, congrats on your highest scoring challenge entry to date.  Looking at your profile, it seems that you are on a steady incline to the top. Camera Work/Technical: This is one of the better moon shots that I have seen in some time.  I like the choice of WB you used here, giving the photo a warmer feel than the normal white moon. Lighting: Great work capturing only the brightness of the moon without overexposing it, and without exposing anything else in the sky. Composition/Content: Wonderful placement to make for a very nice composition. My Opinion: While a very nice capture, moon photos usually don''t do very well in challenges here.  I think this one scored to its full potential on this site.  It is really hard to get a decent composition that affords a size large enough to include the right amount of detail.  Nice capture.', '2006-05-22 00:44:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(2445021, 330297, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Interesting photograph, but this one is very difficult to critique.  I disagree with rick13601.  It does NOT suck.  It is a photo that typically doesn''t do well in challenges here, but it doesn''t suck.  I do like the leading lines, and the light shapes that closely mimic each other do work to draw the viewer into and around the frame.  To do well in these challenges, you need crisply-focused subjects with vibrant colors.  Also, Neat Image seems to help out a lot.  Don''t be discouraged by lower scores and crappy comments.  Some day that''s the way the ball rolls.  I read your posting in the forum earlier, and I agree that every comment is helpful.  Welcome to DPC, I like your attitude, and I look forward to many more entries from you.</td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  Interesting photograph, but this one is very difficult to critique.  I disagree with rick13601.  It does NOT suck.  It is a photo that typically doesn''t do well in challenges here, but it doesn''t suck.  I do like the leading lines, and the light shapes that closely mimic each other do work to draw the viewer into and around the frame.  To do well in these challenges, you need crisply-focused subjects with vibrant colors.  Also, Neat Image seems to help out a lot.  Don''t be discouraged by lower scores and crappy comments.  Some day that''s the way the ball rolls.  I read your posting in the forum earlier, and I agree that every comment is helpful.  Welcome to DPC, I like your attitude, and I look forward to many more entries from you.', '2006-05-22 00:57:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2445149, 329737, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> Nice choice of WB here to establish a warm tone.  It really adds to the mood of the capture.  I can''t put my finger on it, but something seems a bit off focus.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> I like what you accomplished here with the single candle light.  It produces some very nice highlights and shadows on the face.  The flame itself is just a tad overexposed.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> The face is a bit centered, but it works well with the candle balancing the photo from the right side.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> Very nice capture, and I think that this one scored to its full potential.  It meets the challenge very well.<br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  Camera Work/Technical: Nice choice of WB here to establish a warm tone.  It really adds to the mood of the capture.  I can''t put my finger on it, but something seems a bit off focus. Lighting: I like what you accomplished here with the single candle light.  It produces some very nice highlights and shadows on the face.  The flame itself is just a tad overexposed. Composition/Content: The face is a bit centered, but it works well with the candle balancing the photo from the right side. My Opinion: Very nice capture, and I think that this one scored to its full potential.  It meets the challenge very well.', '2006-05-22 01:27:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(2445563, 332168, 39484, '<td valign="top">Great job on this one fp.</td>', 'Great job on this one fp.', '2006-05-22 04:45:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2445566, 332150, 39484, '<td valign="top">Good job breaking your high score.</td>', 'Good job breaking your high score.', '2006-05-22 04:46:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2445586, 329909, 54882, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the nice comments and thanks to TooCool for his advice.<br/>The more i look at this picture the more i realise that it is rather bland , but its been my highest challenge score so far so im pretty pleased i seem to be heading in the correct direction.<br/>The really annoying thing is that i took a picture earlier when it was still early evening and i managed to capture nothing until i looked at the pic and saw 5 or 6 very small streaks of lightning protruding out of the clouds just above the rooflines.</td>', 'Thanks for the nice comments and thanks to TooCool for his advice.The more i look at this picture the more i realise that it is rather bland , but its been my highest challenge score so far so im pretty pleased i seem to be heading in the correct direction.The really annoying thing is that i took a picture earlier when it was still early evening and i managed to capture nothing until i looked at the pic and saw 5 or 6 very small streaks of lightning protruding out of the clouds just above the rooflines.', '2006-05-22 05:15:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2445832, 332219, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯) </b> <br/> <br/>This is a very hard photograph for me to point out anything that''s remotley wrong in the image. Your camera settings could not have been better adjusted, allowing for a beautiful image of the golden colours, crisp focus, and slick headlight trails. As you''ve probably read from your comments, this is certainly worthy to be in the top 5 and I was suprised when it missed. I think the voters in this competition may have been looking more for subject that they can see all of, although that can hardly be done with the angle you used to capture the bridge. It''s unfortunate that the photo isn''t quite central as I think that the ornate building in the background would have looked perfecty with a straight bridge leading up to it; however, the POV you used did help to bring out a little more of the depth and helps to enhance some of the details that could otherwise be lost.<br/> <br/>The light in the top left also keeps the viewers a little distracted but it was rather unavoidable. I think the only real reason that you didn''t achieve a higher place was your focus on the subject of this image. It''s hard to tell if it was the bridge or the building in the background and so our eyes roam over it not quite knowing where to look to judge the picture as a whole.<br/> <br/>Overall, I think that there is very little that can be said about improving this image. You''ve implemented the lighting a angle perfectly with excellent use of your Nikon.<br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions! <br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·.</b></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)   This is a very hard photograph for me to point out anything that''s remotley wrong in the image. Your camera settings could not have been better adjusted, allowing for a beautiful image of the golden colours, crisp focus, and slick headlight trails. As you''ve probably read from your comments, this is certainly worthy to be in the top 5 and I was suprised when it missed. I think the voters in this competition may have been looking more for subject that they can see all of, although that can hardly be done with the angle you used to capture the bridge. It''s unfortunate that the photo isn''t quite central as I think that the ornate building in the background would have looked perfecty with a straight bridge leading up to it; however, the POV you used did help to bring out a little more of the depth and helps to enhance some of the details that could otherwise be lost. The light in the top left also keeps the viewers a little distracted but it was rather unavoidable. I think the only real reason that you didn''t achieve a higher place was your focus on the subject of this image. It''s hard to tell if it was the bridge or the building in the background and so our eyes roam over it not quite knowing where to look to judge the picture as a whole. Overall, I think that there is very little that can be said about improving this image. You''ve implemented the lighting a angle perfectly with excellent use of your Nikon. Good Luck for all Future Competitions!  .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·.', '2006-05-22 08:31:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(2446043, 330036, 16636, '<td valign="top">Awesome shot Ian!!</td>', 'Awesome shot Ian!!', '2006-05-22 09:52:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(2446516, 329501, 22689, '<td valign="top"><b>From the Critique Club :</b> <br/> <br/>¡ Hola Luis !<br/> <br/>Me alegro mucho de que me haya tocado comentar esta foto (he ingresado recientemente en el Club de la Crítica).<br/>¡Vaya momento más bonito elegiste para sacar esta foto! El cielo está genial .<br/>Lo primero intenta aprovechar el tamaño máximo de 640 píxeles , mucha gente aquí resta puntos a la hora de votar cuando ven una foto más pequeña.<br/>En cuanto a la composición , creo que habría ganado bastante si hubieras incluido más vista de la ciudad , con todas esas pequeñas luces.<br/>En los comentarios que te dejaron mencionan que quizá habría quedado mejor sin la luna , ¿te habías planteado recortar la foto por arriba? La verdad , creo que cambia bastante ya que la luna aparece muy brillante y atrae la vista hacia ese punto.<br/>No sé que programa utilizas para editar las fotos pero hay un programita gratuito , el Neat Image que ayuda con la reducción de ruido , otros usuarios prefieren el Noise Ninja pero es de pago.La verdad que resultan muy utiles para disimular el ruido , se puede afinar bastante con los parámetros.<br/> <br/>Es una bonita foto.<br/>Espero ver pronto más fotos tuyas.<br/> <br/>Mambe<br/> <br/>P.D. En los comentarios que te dejan , habrás visto una pequeña casilla que puedes marcar si consideras que dicho comentario te ha sido de ayuda , yo los marco todos <i>helpful</i> salvo los ofensivos o absurdos por supuesto , creo que así la gente se anima más para dejar comentarios.</td>', 'From the Critique Club :  ¡ Hola Luis ! Me alegro mucho de que me haya tocado comentar esta foto (he ingresado recientemente en el Club de la Crítica).¡Vaya momento más bonito elegiste para sacar esta foto! El cielo está genial .Lo primero intenta aprovechar el tamaño máximo de 640 píxeles , mucha gente aquí resta puntos a la hora de votar cuando ven una foto más pequeña.En cuanto a la composición , creo que habría ganado bastante si hubieras incluido más vista de la ciudad , con todas esas pequeñas luces.En los comentarios que te dejaron mencionan que quizá habría quedado mejor sin la luna , ¿te habías planteado recortar la foto por arriba? La verdad , creo que cambia bastante ya que la luna aparece muy brillante y atrae la vista hacia ese punto.No sé que programa utilizas para editar las fotos pero hay un programita gratuito , el Neat Image que ayuda con la reducción de ruido , otros usuarios prefieren el Noise Ninja pero es de pago.La verdad que resultan muy utiles para disimular el ruido , se puede afinar bastante con los parámetros. Es una bonita foto.Espero ver pronto más fotos tuyas. Mambe P.D. En los comentarios que te dejan , habrás visto una pequeña casilla que puedes marcar si consideras que dicho comentario te ha sido de ayuda , yo los marco todos helpful salvo los ofensivos o absurdos por supuesto , creo que así la gente se anima más para dejar comentarios.', '2006-05-22 13:36:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2446626, 332151, 47933, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club Gennaro!<br/> <br/>First impression : Beautiful landscape and I certainly like the sky. I am also fond of the crop.<br/> <br/>As far as technicals go, a longer shutter might have brought out a bit more. Voters here need that "pop" to justify them handing out high scores. If you''d have a captured the moon, some stars or some far off lights you''d have definitely done better. There''s just not a lot of booming detail.<br/> <br/>Overall a beautiful, serene shot tat you should be proud of.<br/> <br/>Thanks for the opportunity to critique this image Gennaro!<br/> <br/>Cheers - Phil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Gennaro! First impression : Beautiful landscape and I certainly like the sky. I am also fond of the crop. As far as technicals go, a longer shutter might have brought out a bit more. Voters here need that "pop" to justify them handing out high scores. If you''d have a captured the moon, some stars or some far off lights you''d have definitely done better. There''s just not a lot of booming detail. Overall a beautiful, serene shot tat you should be proud of. Thanks for the opportunity to critique this image Gennaro! Cheers - Phil', '2006-05-22 14:17:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(2446918, 330960, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯) </b> <br/> <br/>This photograph is incredibly well taken and implemented and I can barely think of anything wrong with it. I think your use of your camera is excellent and couldn''t have been photographed better. The colours in particular are especially brought out by your 15 second exposure which was certainly a wise choice.<br/> <br/>There are just a couple of composition elements that I think may have been slightly improved. The reflection on the first ''trident'' of the sculpture distracts the viewer from those amazing colours and slightly ruins the dark and cool mood that this image conveys. The light on the left in the foreground is also a distracting element that could be removed by placing your tripod at a slightly different POV.<br/> <br/>Otherwise, great DOF, great camera expertise and you should certainly be happy with your 4th place.<br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions! <br/> <br/><b>.·´¯`·-&gt;Lucien&lt;-·´¯`·. </b> <br/></td>', '(¯`·._.·[Critique Club]·._.·´¯)   This photograph is incredibly well taken and implemented and I can barely think of anything wrong with it. I think your use of your camera is excellent and couldn''t have been photographed better. The colours in particular are especially brought out by your 15 second exposure which was certainly a wise choice. There are just a couple of composition elements that I think may have been slightly improved. The reflection on the first ''trident'' of the sculpture distracts the viewer from those amazing colours and slightly ruins the dark and cool mood that this image conveys. The light on the left in the foreground is also a distracting element that could be removed by placing your tripod at a slightly different POV. Otherwise, great DOF, great camera expertise and you should certainly be happy with your 4th place. Good Luck for all Future Competitions!  .·´¯`·->Lucien<-·´¯`·.  ', '2006-05-22 16:37:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2446994, 331987, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi KiwiPix :)<br/> <br/>City skyline photos at night over water usually make excellent images.  The lights and the reflection create some excellent postcard images dappled with color.  This shot has a lot of those qualities, but in my opinion, there is simply too much negative (or unused) space within the frame.  Maybe a tighter composition with a longer lens would create more impact in this image.  Since I''m not familiar with Wellington, there is nothing within this image that identifies your city from any other city that might be photographed this way.  This image is more about light and some possible texture rather than a portrait of a city, in my opinion.  I challenge you to reshoot this photo and fill your frame with some of this beautiful light and reflection :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi KiwiPix :) City skyline photos at night over water usually make excellent images.  The lights and the reflection create some excellent postcard images dappled with color.  This shot has a lot of those qualities, but in my opinion, there is simply too much negative (or unused) space within the frame.  Maybe a tighter composition with a longer lens would create more impact in this image.  Since I''m not familiar with Wellington, there is nothing within this image that identifies your city from any other city that might be photographed this way.  This image is more about light and some possible texture rather than a portrait of a city, in my opinion.  I challenge you to reshoot this photo and fill your frame with some of this beautiful light and reflection :) John Setzler', '2006-05-22 16:57:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447395, 331581, 7969, '<td valign="top">Hi Greetings from the Critique Club,<br/>Hmm, well I think it may be a little more successful had you shot on a little lhigher shutter speed so that one it would be a little more in focus and 2 so that the brightest part of the shot would not be so overexposed. I think fire is a difficult subject, as I think as humans we have a love hate relationship with it.  We are drawn to it, yet it is dangerous when out of control.  I like the dark space on the top of the photo.  My biggest criticism is just that it is not particularly ''wow'' . My favorite part of the shot is the top middle where it is balck but looks like there is something in there still, sort of mysterious.<br/> <br/>Sorry this is not my most helpful critic. Post in forum if this was not helpful enough.<br/>Keep up the efforts!<br/>Lynn</td>', 'Hi Greetings from the Critique Club,Hmm, well I think it may be a little more successful had you shot on a little lhigher shutter speed so that one it would be a little more in focus and 2 so that the brightest part of the shot would not be so overexposed. I think fire is a difficult subject, as I think as humans we have a love hate relationship with it.  We are drawn to it, yet it is dangerous when out of control.  I like the dark space on the top of the photo.  My biggest criticism is just that it is not particularly ''wow'' . My favorite part of the shot is the top middle where it is balck but looks like there is something in there still, sort of mysterious. Sorry this is not my most helpful critic. Post in forum if this was not helpful enough.Keep up the efforts!Lynn', '2006-05-22 19:20:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447646, 331809, 7969, '<td valign="top">Hi greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Interesting choice here, not a typique ''holy reverent'' sort of feel, more dark and mysterious, like maybe the local anarchist or KKK, come here after hours.  In that sense not sure how much it ''fits'' challenge. <br/>But I do think it is emotive shot, I think the tones are good and the lighting is nice, I like the prescence of the moon and in my opinion it probably makes the shot, that along with the missing boards on the church. <br/>I see a couple tiny white flecks in the sky, not sure if it is e or the shot, I think they should get cloned out.<br/> <br/>Good creative take on subject.<br/>Nicely done<br/>lynn</td>', 'Hi greetings from the Critique Club. Interesting choice here, not a typique ''holy reverent'' sort of feel, more dark and mysterious, like maybe the local anarchist or KKK, come here after hours.  In that sense not sure how much it ''fits'' challenge. But I do think it is emotive shot, I think the tones are good and the lighting is nice, I like the prescence of the moon and in my opinion it probably makes the shot, that along with the missing boards on the church. I see a couple tiny white flecks in the sky, not sure if it is e or the shot, I think they should get cloned out. Good creative take on subject.Nicely donelynn', '2006-05-22 20:46:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447670, 332210, 30982, '<td valign="top">This is a GREAT vantage point for a night shot!  The composition is excellent.  You really should try this again and take it with several differing exposure lengths.  Night shots tend to require less light than the camera tells you it needs.</td>', 'This is a GREAT vantage point for a night shot!  The composition is excellent.  You really should try this again and take it with several differing exposure lengths.  Night shots tend to require less light than the camera tells you it needs.', '2006-05-22 20:57:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447699, 332352, 15204, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club,<br/> <br/>This is a tough one for me to critique. I have spent a long time looking at it, and I am struggling to put my finger on exactly what I would suggest to improve it.  Here are my thoughts ...<br/> <br/>This is very nicely exposed for a night shot. I like the details in both the highlights and the shadows.  It is nice and sharp, even with the long exposure.  <br/> <br/>I like the light coming in from the side and below.  It has an eerie quality, which seems appropriate for an empty playground at night.  Kind of creepy.  <br/> <br/>I like the idea of having the slide come down and meet the bottom corner, but I feel like the composition is a bit unbalanced.  The empty space on the right side doesn''t seem like effective use of negative space to me.  The interesting parts of the image are the lines of the bars and they are all clustered on the left side but somehow I find my eye staring out into the emptiness over on the right.  It is hard to explain, but it lessens the impact of the iamge for me. The best suggestion I can think of would be to somehow crop in tighter without losing the long leading line of the slide.  <br/> <br/>Cheers,<br/>Liza</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club, This is a tough one for me to critique. I have spent a long time looking at it, and I am struggling to put my finger on exactly what I would suggest to improve it.  Here are my thoughts ... This is very nicely exposed for a night shot. I like the details in both the highlights and the shadows.  It is nice and sharp, even with the long exposure.   I like the light coming in from the side and below.  It has an eerie quality, which seems appropriate for an empty playground at night.  Kind of creepy.   I like the idea of having the slide come down and meet the bottom corner, but I feel like the composition is a bit unbalanced.  The empty space on the right side doesn''t seem like effective use of negative space to me.  The interesting parts of the image are the lines of the bars and they are all clustered on the left side but somehow I find my eye staring out into the emptiness over on the right.  It is hard to explain, but it lessens the impact of the iamge for me. The best suggestion I can think of would be to somehow crop in tighter without losing the long leading line of the slide.   Cheers,Liza', '2006-05-22 21:07:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447747, 331927, 15204, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club,<br/> <br/>I read your self-critique. I think you were much too hard on yourself.  THis is a very nice night shot.<br/> <br/>I think you sdid a great job with the flare, especially with basic editing.  I like your perspective and the way the horizon meets the end of the dock.<br/> <br/>COmpositionally, I agree with your comments. I would have cropped right to the post on the left, so that the dock posts frame the shot. This would also make the biggest light less centered inthe frame.  You could crop a little off the bottom without changing much if you want3ed to keep the horizontal aspect ratio.<br/> <br/>Depending on the rest of the scene (that is outside of the frame here) it might have been better to take a few steps to your right and angle in a little so that the dock cuts more across the image.  I like the leading lines of the dock and the vertical lines of the poles, and I think angling the dock even more than you did would make it even more dramatic.<br/> <br/>I didn''t vote in this challenge, but I would have given this a 6.  I think it is well executed and interesting to look at.<br/> <br/>Please feel free to pm me with any questions about my comments. <br/> <br/>Cheers,<br/>Liza</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club, I read your self-critique. I think you were much too hard on yourself.  THis is a very nice night shot. I think you sdid a great job with the flare, especially with basic editing.  I like your perspective and the way the horizon meets the end of the dock. COmpositionally, I agree with your comments. I would have cropped right to the post on the left, so that the dock posts frame the shot. This would also make the biggest light less centered inthe frame.  You could crop a little off the bottom without changing much if you want3ed to keep the horizontal aspect ratio. Depending on the rest of the scene (that is outside of the frame here) it might have been better to take a few steps to your right and angle in a little so that the dock cuts more across the image.  I like the leading lines of the dock and the vertical lines of the poles, and I think angling the dock even more than you did would make it even more dramatic. I didn''t vote in this challenge, but I would have given this a 6.  I think it is well executed and interesting to look at. Please feel free to pm me with any questions about my comments.  Cheers,Liza', '2006-05-22 21:21:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447953, 332475, 41337, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/><b>Technicals:</b> <br/>In terms of lighting and color it''s very good. This is a very sharp looking image. In general this image captured night quite well.<br/> <br/><b>Subject/composition:</b> <br/>When I first saw this I didn''t think this was a bridge since the main focus is the building structure. This would have been a better image had you shot this landscape style with the structure to the left. That way you would have the main focal point off-center and also include more of the bridge in the shot for people to identify with. <br/> <br/>Another thing is the size of the image. It''s quite small. Images like that are always going to be at a disadvantage since detail is harder to see and appreciate.<br/> <br/><b>Verdict/Summary:</b> <br/>Overall this image has a lot of quality to it. Probably the best I''ve seen for an image of this size in the challenges and definitely one that scored so low that met the challenge. So good job there. If the image was larger and composed a bit differently I think this would have scored much much higher. That''s all I have. Good luck on your next challenge and I look forward to seeing more of your work!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   Technicals: In terms of lighting and color it''s very good. This is a very sharp looking image. In general this image captured night quite well. Subject/composition: When I first saw this I didn''t think this was a bridge since the main focus is the building structure. This would have been a better image had you shot this landscape style with the structure to the left. That way you would have the main focal point off-center and also include more of the bridge in the shot for people to identify with.  Another thing is the size of the image. It''s quite small. Images like that are always going to be at a disadvantage since detail is harder to see and appreciate. Verdict/Summary: Overall this image has a lot of quality to it. Probably the best I''ve seen for an image of this size in the challenges and definitely one that scored so low that met the challenge. So good job there. If the image was larger and composed a bit differently I think this would have scored much much higher. That''s all I have. Good luck on your next challenge and I look forward to seeing more of your work!', '2006-05-22 22:38:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2447962, 331093, 41337, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/>Ok this may be hard to give a critique since I''d rather you be critiquing my images. :) But here goes...<br/> <br/><b>Technicals:</b> <br/>Flawless. The lighting is impeccable. MAYBE if you shot this a bit more underexposed in RAW you could have fine tuned the hot spots better, but that''s a major nitpick. The color is fantastic. I don''t think you could have treated the color any better to convey night.<br/> <br/><b>Subject/composition:</b> <br/>Excellent choice in subject. It stands out so well in the challenge. Composition wise I can''t envision a better angle. Maybe there was but you pulled this off so well I almost don''t want to see if there''s a better way to show off this structure. <br/> <br/><b>Verdict/Summary:</b> <br/>A winner. Not just a winner in the challenge but a winner as a standalone photograph. Congrats on your multiple ribbons and for pulling off this striking image.<br/> <br/>Edited for spelling.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-22 21:48:59.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   Ok this may be hard to give a critique since I''d rather you be critiquing my images. :) But here goes... Technicals: Flawless. The lighting is impeccable. MAYBE if you shot this a bit more underexposed in RAW you could have fine tuned the hot spots better, but that''s a major nitpick. The color is fantastic. I don''t think you could have treated the color any better to convey night. Subject/composition: Excellent choice in subject. It stands out so well in the challenge. Composition wise I can''t envision a better angle. Maybe there was but you pulled this off so well I almost don''t want to see if there''s a better way to show off this structure.  Verdict/Summary: A winner. Not just a winner in the challenge but a winner as a standalone photograph. Congrats on your multiple ribbons and for pulling off this striking image. Edited for spelling.', '2006-05-22 22:46:15.000000', '2006-05-22 22:48:59.000000', 0),
(2448108, 331899, 59410, '<td valign="top">wow! this is a gorgeous photo! i love the effect of the water.... it reminds me of something i have seen before<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-22 22:43:58.</i></td>', 'wow! this is a gorgeous photo! i love the effect of the water.... it reminds me of something i have seen before', '2006-05-22 23:43:21.000000', '2006-05-22 23:43:58.000000', 0),
(2448742, 330607, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> Everything in the frame is nicely focused and nicely exposed.  The lights are just a bit blown out, but nothing outside what you''d expect with a night photograph.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> This one is nicely lit.  It really provides for a very calm, peaceful mood.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> This area is lacking in your capture.  As rick13601 pointed out, there really is nothing that grabs the eye.  It just looks open and peaceful, but lacks a real point of interest.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> I believe that this one scored pretty well for what you captured.  Technically, you have a fine image.  Great lighting, great exposure and great BW post-processing.  With a more defined subject, this one would have scored much, much better.  It fits the challenge very well, and the title compliments it perfectly.<br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique. Camera Work/Technical: Everything in the frame is nicely focused and nicely exposed.  The lights are just a bit blown out, but nothing outside what you''d expect with a night photograph. Lighting: This one is nicely lit.  It really provides for a very calm, peaceful mood. Composition/Content: This area is lacking in your capture.  As rick13601 pointed out, there really is nothing that grabs the eye.  It just looks open and peaceful, but lacks a real point of interest. My Opinion: I believe that this one scored pretty well for what you captured.  Technically, you have a fine image.  Great lighting, great exposure and great BW post-processing.  With a more defined subject, this one would have scored much, much better.  It fits the challenge very well, and the title compliments it perfectly.', '2006-05-23 03:50:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2448780, 332009, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> This one is tough to critique fairly because nothing seems to be in focus.  If that was the intent of the capture, including that info in the comments section would be helpful for the critique.  I will add that I really like the WB that you chose.  You captured a photograph that projects a really great mood.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> I think it''s nicely lit, but I would have liked to have seen the exposure just a bit longer.  This would have included more detail.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> Very well done.  I like the way you composed this one so that the frame is split between the dark and the light.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> Great idea, just not fully executed.  With better focus and a bit more detail, this one would have scored a great deal higher.<br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique. Camera Work/Technical: This one is tough to critique fairly because nothing seems to be in focus.  If that was the intent of the capture, including that info in the comments section would be helpful for the critique.  I will add that I really like the WB that you chose.  You captured a photograph that projects a really great mood. Lighting: I think it''s nicely lit, but I would have liked to have seen the exposure just a bit longer.  This would have included more detail. Composition/Content: Very well done.  I like the way you composed this one so that the frame is split between the dark and the light. My Opinion: Great idea, just not fully executed.  With better focus and a bit more detail, this one would have scored a great deal higher.', '2006-05-23 03:58:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2448824, 330785, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> Well chosen settings to produce a nice, high-scoring photograph.  I really like the WB here, as it rendered near-perfect tones.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> Impecable.  You captured great lighting in all areas of the shot.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> Well seen, well composed and well captured.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> I think you have a great image here, but it is a hair over processed.  It looks like just a bit too much neat image.<br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  First off, always try to include some info about the shot in the Photographer''s Comments section. It really helps out when we know what the photographer was thinking and what equipment/lighting/etc. were used, especially when you request a critique. Camera Work/Technical: Well chosen settings to produce a nice, high-scoring photograph.  I really like the WB here, as it rendered near-perfect tones. Lighting: Impecable.  You captured great lighting in all areas of the shot. Composition/Content: Well seen, well composed and well captured. My Opinion: I think you have a great image here, but it is a hair over processed.  It looks like just a bit too much neat image.', '2006-05-23 04:16:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(2448920, 332370, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯) </b> <br/> <br/>Ok, some of what I say will have been repeated in the comments already written, but I''ll try to come up with a few original points and go into a little more detail.<br/> <br/>I think the main problem that limited this image''s rating was that you didn''t really have much in the photograph itself for the viewer to look at. About 80% of it is just a plain sky. The photo itself is a good one and is nice to look at at first, but once you''ve got over the couple of millimetres of lights at the base of the image, and the slightly distracting street light, there isn''t much of a reason to continue to observe your picture. I think that perhaps a few more subjects and maybe less sky would have done the trick.<br/> <br/>In terms of technical elements and your use of your camera, the photograph is sound and your choices for low aperture and long shutter speed certainly paid off, however what remains are those layers of colour that are scattered throughout the sky. This graininess and colour blocking is usually the result of a digital camera trying to overcompensate for dark conditions and this is always made worse by pointing it at a direct source of light which confuses the inner processors. However, from what I''ve seen of the other entries for this competition, this image certainly remains one of the better ones, so I''m surprised that it didn''t achieve a higher place.<br/> <br/>Overall, I''d say that your ability to take such landscape images is fine although after a quick look over your past entries, it certainly looks as though you specialise in those great macro shots.<br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions! <br/> <br/><b>.´¯`-&gt;Lucien&lt;-´¯`. </b> <br/></td>', '(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯)   Ok, some of what I say will have been repeated in the comments already written, but I''ll try to come up with a few original points and go into a little more detail. I think the main problem that limited this image''s rating was that you didn''t really have much in the photograph itself for the viewer to look at. About 80% of it is just a plain sky. The photo itself is a good one and is nice to look at at first, but once you''ve got over the couple of millimetres of lights at the base of the image, and the slightly distracting street light, there isn''t much of a reason to continue to observe your picture. I think that perhaps a few more subjects and maybe less sky would have done the trick. In terms of technical elements and your use of your camera, the photograph is sound and your choices for low aperture and long shutter speed certainly paid off, however what remains are those layers of colour that are scattered throughout the sky. This graininess and colour blocking is usually the result of a digital camera trying to overcompensate for dark conditions and this is always made worse by pointing it at a direct source of light which confuses the inner processors. However, from what I''ve seen of the other entries for this competition, this image certainly remains one of the better ones, so I''m surprised that it didn''t achieve a higher place. Overall, I''d say that your ability to take such landscape images is fine although after a quick look over your past entries, it certainly looks as though you specialise in those great macro shots. Good Luck for all Future Competitions!  .´¯`->Lucien<-´¯`.  ', '2006-05-23 07:06:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(2449051, 331053, 57274, '<td valign="top">great capture, Sandy.  aside from wonderful reflections and magnificent Frost building, being an Austinite (one who also lived in SA), I appreciate this one a lot;)</td>', 'great capture, Sandy.  aside from wonderful reflections and magnificent Frost building, being an Austinite (one who also lived in SA), I appreciate this one a lot;)', '2006-05-23 08:26:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2449056, 331143, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯) </b> <br/> <br/>Well my first impression of your photograph is certainly a great one. The rotational symmetry, crisp focus and perfect colours all work together to make this a perfect image for the challenge and I''m not at all surprised that it landed itself in the top 90%.<br/> <br/>You clearly know how to use your Olympus to achieve the best photograph possible and your low aperture and long shutter speed clearly paid off to allow for a stunning image with a perfect deep, blue sky and a golden ambient lighting.<br/> <br/>As for your composition, there isn''t a lot that could be improved. You did well to keep the photo completely level and manage to capture the tunnel from right in the centre as voters on DPC these days seem to really pick up on that kind of thing. It''s a shame that some of the lights were out along the edges of the walkway, although that is hardly easy to change through means of your own so it certainly shouldn''t limit your score. I think that I agree with some of the previous comments though when it comes to the street clutter at the base of the image. I think the road is fine and adds more of the geometrical imagery by running perpendicular to the bridge, but some of the buildings, especially the one(s) on the right distract the viewer slightly away from the subject. The street lamp just left of the bridge is also a shame, although it does cast that excellent shadow across the floor of the tunnel.<br/> <br/>Overall, your photograph is certainly one of the best in this challenge and I''m surprised it didn''t go further. However, you should be proud of such a beautiful image and of your ability to capture it in the best way.<br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions! <br/> <br/><b>.´¯`-&gt;Lucien&lt;-´¯`. </b> <br/></td>', '(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯)   Well my first impression of your photograph is certainly a great one. The rotational symmetry, crisp focus and perfect colours all work together to make this a perfect image for the challenge and I''m not at all surprised that it landed itself in the top 90%. You clearly know how to use your Olympus to achieve the best photograph possible and your low aperture and long shutter speed clearly paid off to allow for a stunning image with a perfect deep, blue sky and a golden ambient lighting. As for your composition, there isn''t a lot that could be improved. You did well to keep the photo completely level and manage to capture the tunnel from right in the centre as voters on DPC these days seem to really pick up on that kind of thing. It''s a shame that some of the lights were out along the edges of the walkway, although that is hardly easy to change through means of your own so it certainly shouldn''t limit your score. I think that I agree with some of the previous comments though when it comes to the street clutter at the base of the image. I think the road is fine and adds more of the geometrical imagery by running perpendicular to the bridge, but some of the buildings, especially the one(s) on the right distract the viewer slightly away from the subject. The street lamp just left of the bridge is also a shame, although it does cast that excellent shadow across the floor of the tunnel. Overall, your photograph is certainly one of the best in this challenge and I''m surprised it didn''t go further. However, you should be proud of such a beautiful image and of your ability to capture it in the best way. Good Luck for all Future Competitions!  .´¯`->Lucien<-´¯`.  ', '2006-05-23 08:27:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(2449099, 332181, 2978, '<td valign="top">Great work</td>', 'Great work', '2006-05-23 08:40:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2449138, 331098, 2978, '<td valign="top">I''m lost in space now. Well done.</td>', 'I''m lost in space now. Well done.', '2006-05-23 09:00:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2449390, 332215, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯) </b> <br/> <br/>Well this is certainly a worthy photograph for the competition and could certainly have come a lot higher if for a few changes to be made. From this photograph we can clearly see that you know how to use your Cannon to achieve the optimum results for your composition. Your 30 second shutter speed was certainly a good decision to bring out those reflections on the water.<br/> <br/>I think that this photo didn''t go further because of its lack of interesting subjects. There is too much black empty space at the bottom of the image, yet the sky looks so interesting, it''s a shame that you didn''t show more of that instead. Otherwise the photo is a great idea and produces a nice image with heat.<br/> <br/>Overall, I''m surprised that this picture didn''t actually go further but don''t take it personally. From browsing through your profile it would appear that your macro shots seem to be much more effective. <br/> <br/>Good Luck for all Future Competitions! <br/> <br/><b>.´¯`-&gt;Lucien&lt;-´¯`. </b> <br/></td>', '(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯)   Well this is certainly a worthy photograph for the competition and could certainly have come a lot higher if for a few changes to be made. From this photograph we can clearly see that you know how to use your Cannon to achieve the optimum results for your composition. Your 30 second shutter speed was certainly a good decision to bring out those reflections on the water. I think that this photo didn''t go further because of its lack of interesting subjects. There is too much black empty space at the bottom of the image, yet the sky looks so interesting, it''s a shame that you didn''t show more of that instead. Otherwise the photo is a great idea and produces a nice image with heat. Overall, I''m surprised that this picture didn''t actually go further but don''t take it personally. From browsing through your profile it would appear that your macro shots seem to be much more effective.  Good Luck for all Future Competitions!  .´¯`->Lucien<-´¯`.  ', '2006-05-23 11:13:32.000000', NULL, 0),
